.TH VM_FAULT 9 "January 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Developer's Manual"
.SH NAME
vm_fault \- high-level page fault handler
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/vm/vm_fault.h>
.LP
.BI "int vm_fault(vm_map_t *" map ", uintptr_t " va ", uint8_t " prot ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vm_fault
resolves a page fault at virtual address
.I va
within the given
.I map .
It enforces protection checks specified by
.I prot
and installs valid mappings into the physical map (pmap).
.SS Fault Resolution
1. Locates the
.B vm_map_entry
covering
.I va .
.br
2. Verifies that the requested access
.I prot
is allowed by the entry's protection.
.br
3. Traverses the backing object chain (shadow chain) to find the page.
.br
4. Handles Copy-on-Write (COW) if writing to a shared page.
.br
5. Allocates a new page (zero-filled or paged in) if not found.
.br
6. Enters the physical mapping into the pmap.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns
.B VM_FAULT_SUCCESS
(0) if resolved successfully.
.br
Returns
.B VM_FAULT_ERROR
(-1) on failure (invalid address, protection violation, OOM).
.br
Returns
.B VM_FAULT_RETRY
(1) if the fault should be retried (e.g., transient lock failure).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vm_map (9),
.BR vm_object (9),
.BR pmap (9)
